# E-1268: verbs.jsonl is append-only — `merge=union` makes git auto-concat
# both sides' new lines on rebase/merge, eliminating the trailing-region
# conflicts that plagued the JSON-array format. Same-`value` duplicates
# produced by union merge are absorbed silently by the load-time dedup in
# matchers._add_verb_to_file.
.endless/verbs.jsonl merge=union
# E-2000: `.endless/LESSONS.md` — the corrections log — is append-only in normal
# use: every session adds an entry at the end. `merge=union` keeps both sides
# instead of conflicting when two task branches each record a lesson before
# either lands. Measured from pristine fixtures: two branches appending
# different entries merge clean with no duplication, and an identical entry on
# both sides is kept once (git sees one change, not a conflict).
#
# Differs from verbs.jsonl above in one way worth knowing: that file has a
# load-time dedup reader (matchers._add_verb_to_file) to absorb union's
# duplicates. This one's only reader is a human, so there is no dedup pass. The
# residual hazard is therefore NOT appends but concurrent edits to the SAME
# line — two branches rewriting the header both survive, silently, one after
# the other. Header edits are rare and land visibly in review; entries are the
# common case and are safe.
#
# NOT applied to `.endless/db-ledger/*.jsonl`, where concatenating both sides
# would duplicate DB mutation records. That directory avoids conflicts by
# sharding filenames per machine instead, so no two writers share a path.
.endless/LESSONS.md merge=union
